cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use ASLA in your research, please cite it."
title: "ASLA: Algorithm-Selection Leaderboard Audit"
type: software
authors:
  - name: "SAID Laboratory"
abstract: >-
  ASLA audits whether small-compute scaling-law projections select the same
  training intervention that measured target-budget runs would select, and
  formalises the question as transductive pure exploration: the arms trained
  during exploration differ from the target-budget arms about which a decision
  must be made. On DataDecide, no estimator, objective or compute allocation
  we tested beats ranking at the largest single fitted scale, including a
  cost-constrained decision-optimal design evaluated at matched compute. Under
  the linear model, designing for the decision and designing for target-region
  fit provably coincide, so the failure is localised to the model rather than
  the objective. The constructive result is a delta-PAC selection rule with
  abstention, which certifies most orderings at the top of the ladder with no
  certified errors while showing that most small-scale leaderboard orderings
  cannot be certified at any affordable seed budget. The package provides
  decision-level metrics with bootstrap confidence intervals, FDR-controlled
  crossover detection, misspecification-aware ensemble projections with an
  extrapolation reliability score, cost-aware sequential selection (racing),
  conformal projection intervals, transductive experimental design, and a
  parameterized benchmark (ASLA-Bench) with known ground truth.
keywords:
  - scaling laws
  - model selection
  - transductive bandits
  - pure exploration
  - experimental design
  - reproducibility
repository-code: "https://github.com/esaran1/algorithm-selection-audit"
